RAWMSTAT(1)

# NAME

rawmstat - configurable status producer for rawm

# SYNOPSIS

*rawmstat* [*-p*] [*-c* _path_]

*rawmstat* *-C* [*-c* _path_]

*rawmstat* *-v*

# DESCRIPTION

*rawmstat* executes configured status blocks, composes their textual output,
and publishes the result for *rawm*(1).

With the *rawm-v1* protocol, the composed status is written as plain UTF-8 to
the *_RAWM_STATUS_V1* property of the X root window.  The property type is
*UTF8_STRING*.  No process discovery, socket, root *WM_NAME* convention, or
in-band presentation markup is involved.

Configuration is read at run time.  See *rawmstat.conf*(5) for configuration
precedence, block semantics, update intervals, and realtime update signals.

Unless *-p* is used, rawmstat requires an X display.  It does not start or
supervise *rawm*(1); normally both processes are started by the X session.

# OPTIONS

*-c* _path_, *--config* _path_
	Load built-in defaults followed by exactly _path_.  The normal system and
	user configuration files are not read.

*-C*, *--check-config*
	Load and validate the effective configuration, then exit without opening
	the X display or executing any blocks.

*-p*, *--stdout*
	Write each changed composed status line to standard output instead of the
	X root property.  This is useful for testing and for text-oriented status
	consumers.  The process continues running and updates according to the same
	interval and signal rules as X mode.

*-v*, *--version*
	Print the version and exit.

*-h*, *--help*
	Print a short usage message and exit.

# RUNTIME MODEL

At startup every configured block is executed once.  A block with a non-zero
*interval* is subsequently executed on its monotonic interval.  A block with a
non-zero *signal* may also be refreshed by sending the corresponding realtime
signal to the rawmstat process.  Signal handlers only wake the main event loop;
commands and Xlib operations are not executed from signal context.

Block commands are direct argument vectors executed with *execvp*(3).  Shell
syntax is not interpreted unless a shell is explicitly configured, for
example:

```
command = ("/bin/sh", "-c", "printf '%s' \"$HOME\"");
```

Only the first output line from a block is used.  A trailing carriage return
is removed.  Block text, prefixes, delimiters, and the final status must be
valid UTF-8 and the final *rawm-v1* payload must fit within 255 bytes.

# SIGNALS

*SIGINT*, *SIGTERM*
	Terminate the main loop cleanly.

*SIGRTMIN+N*
	Refresh each block whose *signal* setting is _N_.  A signal value of zero
	disables signal-triggered refresh for that block.  Availability of realtime
	signal offsets is validated at startup.

# FILES

_/etc/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	System configuration, when present.

_$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	User configuration when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is set to an absolute path.

_$HOME/.config/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	User configuration fallback.

# EXIT STATUS

*0* on normal termination or successful configuration validation; *>0* on a
configuration, display, setup, or runtime publication error.

# SEE ALSO

*rawm*(1), *rawm.conf*(5), *rawmstat.conf*(5), *libconfig*(5)

